Disaster Recovery Capability Assessment
Infrastructure as Code vs Complete DR Solution
Santander Corporate & Investment Banking
Cloud Architecture Team
February 2026
Classification: Internal – Technical Analysis

Table of Contents



Executive Summary
Key Finding: Harness is NOT a Complete DR Solution
Harness is a sophisticated Infrastructure as Code Management (IaCM) and continuous delivery platform that automates infrastructure provisioning and application deployment. However, Harness does not provide—and does not claim to provide—the complete set of capabilities required for operational disaster recovery and multi-region resilience as defined by regulatory frameworks including DORA and industry best practices.
The critical distinction: Harness can deploy infrastructure across multiple regions (Infrastructure Deployment), but it cannot ensure service continuity during regional failures (Operational Resilience). This distinction is fundamental and often misunderstood by stakeholders who conflate the ability to provision infrastructure with the capability to maintain operational continuity.
What Harness Actually Provides
Harness excels at infrastructure automation and deployment orchestration. The platform provides Infrastructure as Code management with Terraform, OpenTofu, and CloudFormation support, GitOps-based continuous delivery enabling declarative infrastructure definitions, pipeline automation for deploying applications and infrastructure across multiple clouds and regions, policy-as-code for governance and compliance validation, and drift detection identifying configuration divergence between desired and actual state.
These capabilities are valuable and necessary components of a resilient architecture. Harness enables consistent, repeatable infrastructure provisioning—a prerequisite for multi-region deployment. However, infrastructure provisioning represents only one component of a complete DR strategy.
Critical Gaps for Complete DR
Harness does not provide several essential components mandatory for achieving regulatory compliance with DORA Article 28 requirements and operational resilience targets. Specifically, Harness lacks external health observation infrastructure monitoring application availability from outside the deployment boundary, automated traffic coordination and failover mechanisms routing users to healthy regions, database replication and state synchronization ensuring data consistency across regions, session state management maintaining user continuity during failover, real-time monitoring and alerting for service degradation detection, and automated incident response and remediation workflows.
These gaps are not design flaws—Harness is designed for infrastructure deployment automation, not operational resilience management. Organizations implementing Harness must complement it with additional platforms and services to achieve complete DR capability.
Recommendation
Harness should be evaluated as a component of a comprehensive DR strategy, not as a complete DR solution. For Santander CIB, the recommended approach combines Harness for infrastructure deployment automation with cloud-native services (AWS Route 53, Aurora Global Database, ElastiCache Global Datastore) or specialized DR platforms (CloudEndure, Zerto, Veeam) for operational resilience capabilities. This document provides detailed analysis of what Harness provides, what's missing, and which market solutions address the gaps.

0. Understanding Harness: Company Profile and Product Positioning
0.1 What Harness Is — In Their Own Words
Before evaluating Harness against disaster recovery requirements, it is essential to understand what the company itself says it builds. Harness's official public description, as it appears in search engine knowledge panels and corporate communications, reads:
"Harness is a unified, end-to-end AI software delivery platform to manage the SDLC using purpose-built AI agents."
Every word in this description matters for this analysis. Harness is a software delivery platform. Its purpose is to manage the Software Development Lifecycle — the process of building, testing, securing, and deploying code. It does this using AI-assisted automation. This is a precise and accurate description of the platform, and it is entirely consistent with what the Harness documentation reveals.
The Software Development Lifecycle ends at deployment. It does not extend into the operational domain of keeping deployed systems alive, responding to regional failures, synchronising data across geographies, or executing failover. That domain — operational resilience — is structurally outside what Harness is designed to do.
0.2 The Complete Harness Module Catalogue
The Harness Developer Hub at https://developer.harness.io/docs lists the following modules as of February 2026. Reading this list carefully is instructive: every module addresses a phase of software creation and delivery. None addresses runtime operational resilience or disaster recovery for customer applications.
