Reviewing the full module catalogue makes the picture clear: Harness covers the entire journey from writing code (Code Repository, CI) through deploying it safely (CD, IaCM, Feature Flags) and measuring the result (SRM, Insights). It is a comprehensive toolchain for software delivery. It is not an operational infrastructure resilience platform.
0.3 Important Nuance: Chaos Engineering
Harness does include a Chaos Engineering module, and it deserves honest treatment because it is the module most likely to be cited as evidence that Harness covers DR. Harness Chaos Engineering is documented at https://developer.harness.io/docs/chaos-engineering/ and its own documentation describes it as:
"Harness Chaos Engineering provides end-to-end tooling for resilience testing at enterprise scale through proven chaos engineering principles. Chaos Engineering is the practice of proactively introducing controlled faults into applications and infrastructure to test the resilience of business services."
The key word is testing. Chaos Engineering validates whether your resilience mechanisms work — it does not implement those mechanisms. To use a medical analogy: a stress test on a patient's heart tells you whether the cardiovascular system can handle exertion. It does not itself strengthen the heart. Similarly, Harness Chaos Engineering can confirm that your Route 53 failover triggers correctly, that your Aurora Global Database promotes successfully, and that your sessions survive a regional kill. But it requires those mechanisms to exist first and be built elsewhere.
The Harness Chaos Engineering documentation explicitly describes injecting faults such as pod deletion, node network loss, and availability zone failures. These are test scenarios — probes that check whether your DR architecture responds correctly. They are not substitutes for that architecture. The documentation for Chaos Engineering resilience probes (https://developer.harness.io/docs/chaos-engineering/concepts/explore-concepts/resilience-probes) further confirms this: probes validate your system's health checks and SLOs during fault injection. They depend on health checks being defined and measured externally.
Conclusion on Chaos Engineering: This module is a valuable complement to a complete DR architecture, not a replacement for it. Used well, it would be an excellent addition to the Santander CIB DR strategy — but only after the underlying health observation, failover, and state synchronisation layers are in place.
0.4 Important Nuance: Service Reliability Management
The Service Reliability Management (SRM) module, documented at https://developer.harness.io/docs/service-reliability-management/, provides SLO definition and tracking, error budget management, and deployment-correlated reliability dashboards. Its documentation states:
"Harness Service Reliability Management (SRM) helps engineering and DevOps teams balance feature velocity and bug fixes with the stability and reliability needs of a production environment. You can monitor SLOs, track error budget burndown, and identify change impact."
SRM is a tool for measuring reliability targets and correlating them with deployment events. It ingests metrics from external sources — Prometheus, Datadog, Dynatrace, New Relic — and presents SLO compliance dashboards. It can trigger automated deployment rollbacks when SLO conditions are breached (https://developer.harness.io/docs/service-reliability-management/manage-slo/automated-deployment-rollback/). This is a powerful capability in the context of release management.
However, SRM does not perform external health checking, initiate regional failover, or ensure data replication. It tracks SLO metrics that other systems produce. If there are no health checks configured externally, SRM has nothing to measure. If there is no traffic routing layer, SRM cannot trigger failover. It is a dashboard and measurement layer, not an operational control plane.
Conclusion on SRM: A valuable reliability measurement tool. It could serve as the SLO compliance reporting layer within a complete DR architecture for Santander CIB, satisfying the 'measurable RTO/RPO documentation' requirement regulators need — but only when integrated with live health and traffic data from the actual resilience infrastructure.
0.5 The Critical Mismatch: SDLC vs Operational Resilience
The conceptual gap is now fully visible. Harness's own positioning as an 'AI software delivery platform to manage the SDLC' confirms exactly what this document argues. Disaster recovery is not a phase of the SDLC. It is an operational concern that begins where the SDLC ends — after software has been delivered and is running in production.
A concrete illustration: when AWS Frankfurt fails at 3:00 AM on a Tuesday, no deployment is happening. No CI pipeline is running. No IaC provisioning is in progress. The SDLC is idle. Yet this is precisely the moment when a complete DR system must act — detecting the failure within 30 seconds, routing traffic within 2 minutes, promoting a database replica, and confirming operational continuity. Harness has no role in this sequence because this sequence is not part of software delivery. It is operational infrastructure resilience.
This is not a criticism of Harness. A hammer is not a poor substitute for a screwdriver; they are different tools for different tasks. The risk arises when stakeholders assume that acquiring a world-class SDLC platform simultaneously acquires operational resilience capability. It does not.

1. Harness Platform Capabilities: Detailed Analysis
1.1 Infrastructure as Code Management (IaCM)
Harness IaCM provides comprehensive infrastructure automation capabilities positioning it as a direct competitor to HashiCorp Terraform Cloud, Spacelift, and similar platforms.
1.1.1 Core IaCM Features (Source-Validated)
Terraform and OpenTofu Support: Harness provides native integration with Terraform and OpenTofu enabling organisations to manage infrastructure using familiar tooling. The platform handles Terraform state management, workspace organisation, and execution orchestration.
Documentation: https://developer.harness.io/docs/infrastructure-as-code-management/
Multi-Cloud Support: Harness IaCM supports infrastructure provisioning across AWS, Azure, Google Cloud Platform, and Kubernetes. This enables organisations to define infrastructure templates applicable across providers. Note that multi-cloud infrastructure deployment does not inherently provide multi-cloud operational resilience; applications still require health monitoring, traffic routing, and data replication to achieve fault tolerance.
GitOps Workflow: Harness implements GitOps principles where Git repositories serve as the single source of truth for infrastructure definitions. Changes to infrastructure are proposed via pull requests, reviewed through standard code review processes, and automatically applied upon merge.
Continuous Delivery & GitOps documentation: https://developer.harness.io/docs/continuous-delivery
Policy as Code: Harness integrates with Open Policy Agent (OPA) enabling organisations to define and enforce infrastructure policies covering security requirements, cost constraints, and regulatory data residency rules.
Policy as Code documentation: https://developer.harness.io/docs/platform/governance/policy-as-code/
Drift Detection: Harness IaCM detects configuration drift — divergence between desired state (Git) and actual state (deployed infrastructure). This is relevant to DR preparedness because configuration drift in secondary regions can prevent successful failover.
IaCM overview: https://developer.harness.io/docs/infrastructure-as-code-management/
1.1.2 What IaCM Enables for DR
Harness IaCM enables consistent infrastructure provisioning across regions, ensuring secondary regions have infrastructure parity with primary. It reduces human error risk through automated configuration deployment and provides versioning and rollback for infrastructure-induced incidents. These are necessary preconditions for DR but are not DR mechanisms in themselves.
1.1.3 What IaCM Does NOT Provide for DR
Harness IaCM does not monitor application health or detect regional failures. It cannot redirect user traffic. It does not replicate application data or session state. It does not provide continuous operational monitoring or real-time alerting. These gaps are not configuration shortcomings — they fall outside the functional scope of an infrastructure deployment tool.
1.2 Continuous Delivery and Deployment
Harness CD provides sophisticated pipeline automation for deploying applications across environments and regions.
Continuous Delivery documentation: https://developer.harness.io/docs/continuous-delivery
1.2.1 Deployment Automation Features
Multi-Stage Pipelines: Harness pipelines orchestrate complex workflows including sequential or parallel regional deployments. A pipeline might deploy to Ireland, validate, then deploy to Frankfurt. This enforces deployment consistency but operates during planned changes, not during failures.
Progressive Deployment Strategies: Harness supports blue-green deployments, canary releases with automated rollback based on metrics, and rolling deployments. These strategies reduce deployment risk but are not triggered by infrastructure failures.
Verification and Validation: Harness integrates with Datadog, New Relic, Dynatrace, and Prometheus to validate deployment success. Post-deployment validation can trigger automatic rollback if metrics degrade. This addresses deployment-induced problems, not infrastructure-level incidents.
Platform integrations FAQ: https://developer.harness.io/docs/platform/harness-platform-faqs/
1.2.2 An Important Clarification on Harness 'Disaster Recovery'
A search of the Harness documentation reveals a page titled 'Set up disaster recovery' located at:
Harness DR page: https://developer.harness.io/docs/self-managed-enterprise-edition/advanced-configurations/set-up-disaster-recovery/
This page is located within the Self-Managed Enterprise Edition section and describes how to configure disaster recovery for the Harness platform itself — that is, how to ensure that Harness as a tool remains available if its hosting infrastructure fails. This is disaster recovery of the CI/CD tool, not disaster recovery of applications managed by that tool. This distinction is fundamental and must be clearly communicated to stakeholders: Harness has planned for its own resilience, but this does not confer DR capability onto applications deployed through it.

2. Complete Disaster Recovery Requirements
Achieving operational resilience compliant with DORA Article 28 and industry standards requires implementing all components of a coordinated multi-region architecture. The following analysis maps DR requirements to implementation needs, and explicitly indicates where Harness contributes and where it does not.
2.1 The Three Essential Coordination Layers
As established in Santander CIB's multi-region architecture framework, true operational resilience requires three external coordination layers operating independently from application infrastructure.
