2.2 Detailed Capability Gap Analysis
2.2.1 External Health Observation (NOT PROVIDED)
Requirement: Applications must be monitored from multiple geographic locations outside the infrastructure boundary to detect regional failures, network partitions, DNS issues, and performance degradation. Health checks must execute synthetic user transactions validating end-to-end functionality.
What Harness Provides: Harness does not provide external health monitoring. The platform can verify that infrastructure exists and deployments succeed but cannot monitor whether users can access applications during normal operations. Harness's own 'supported platforms' documentation (https://developer.harness.io/docs/platform/platform-whats-supported/) lists dozens of integration partners but none provide external health check infrastructure equivalent to Route 53 or Pingdom.
What is Needed: Route 53 health checks from AWS global network, Azure Traffic Manager endpoint monitoring, or Datadog Synthetic Monitoring executing real user workflows continuously and independently from any deployment activity.
Route 53 DNS Failover: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/Route53/latest/DeveloperGuide/dns-failover.html
2.2.2 Traffic Coordination and Failover (NOT PROVIDED)
Requirement: When health checks detect regional failure, traffic must automatically route to healthy regions without manual intervention, meeting an RTO target of under 5 minutes for Tier 1 applications.
What Harness Provides: Harness's CD pipelines can deploy load balancers and routing rules, but they execute during planned deployments, not during failure events. Harness cannot detect a runtime failure and trigger a traffic rerouting response.
What is Needed: Route 53 failover routing policies, Azure Traffic Manager, or AWS Global Accelerator — all operating as continuous control planes that respond to health check data in real time.
Azure Traffic Manager: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/traffic-manager/
Cloudflare Load Balancing: https://developers.cloudflare.com/load-balancing/
2.2.3 Database and State Replication (NOT PROVIDED)
Requirement: Application data must replicate continuously across regions with RPO near zero. Session state must synchronise to prevent user session loss during failover.
What Harness Provides: Harness Database DevOps (https://developer.harness.io/docs/database-devops) manages database schema changes and deployment migrations — the DevOps lifecycle of schema versioning. It does not perform runtime data replication across regions. An organisation using Harness Database DevOps can deploy a schema change to both Frankfurt and Dublin databases consistently, but Harness does not replicate the data between them.
What is Needed: Aurora Global Database, Cosmos DB multi-master replication, CockroachDB distributed SQL, or Redis Global Datastore — all providing continuous data replication at the storage layer.
Aurora Global Database: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonRDS/latest/AuroraUserGuide/aurora-global-database.html
CockroachDB Multi-Region: https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/stable/multiregion-overview.html
2.2.4 Operational Monitoring and Alerting (PARTIAL)
Requirement: Real-time monitoring of application performance, error rates, throughput, and latency. Alerting when metrics breach thresholds. Integration with incident management for escalation and automated response.
What Harness Provides: The SRM module (https://developer.harness.io/docs/service-reliability-management/) provides SLO management and error budget tracking. It ingests metrics from Prometheus, Datadog, New Relic, and Dynatrace and presents SLO compliance dashboards. It can trigger deployment rollbacks when SLOs are breached. This is genuinely useful but it is a reporting and measurement layer, not a monitoring engine. SRM depends on those external monitoring platforms to supply the metrics it visualises.
What is Needed: Datadog, New Relic, or cloud-native services (CloudWatch, Azure Monitor) providing continuous metric collection, anomaly detection, and alerting — feeding into PagerDuty or equivalent for incident escalation.
Datadog Infrastructure Monitoring: https://www.datadoghq.com/product/infrastructure-monitoring/
PagerDuty Incident Response: https://www.pagerduty.com/
2.2.5 Automated Incident Response (NOT PROVIDED)
Requirement: Automated runbooks executing failover procedures when failures are detected, without requiring human intervention. Manual procedures are incompatible with RTO targets under 5 minutes.
What Harness Provides: Harness pipelines can theoretically be triggered by external webhooks, but they are designed as deployment orchestrators, not incident responders. There is no built-in failure detection mechanism that would trigger a Harness pipeline in response to a regional outage. The AI SRE module (https://developer.harness.io/docs/ai-sre/) provides AI-assisted incident triage and runbook suggestions, but it assists human responders rather than executing autonomous failover.
What is Needed: AWS Systems Manager Automation Runbooks, Azure Automation, or PagerDuty Process Automation — purpose-built for automated incident response that executes without human intervention.
AWS Systems Manager Automation: https://docs.aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/latest/userguide/systems-manager-automation.html

3. Market Solutions for Complete DR Capability
Multiple vendors provide comprehensive DR solutions addressing all required components. The following analysis examines platforms designed specifically for disaster recovery and operational resilience.
3.1 AWS Native Multi-Region Architecture
Amazon Web Services provides integrated services spanning all DR requirements when applications are hosted entirely on AWS.
	•	Route 53 Health Checks and Failover Routing
  : https://aws.amazon.com/route53/
	•	Aurora Global Database
  : https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/global-database/
	•	ElastiCache Global Datastore
  : https://aws.amazon.com/elasticache/
	•	S3 Cross-Region Replication
  : https://aws.amazon.com/s3/features/replication/
	•	CloudWatch Monitoring and Alarms
  : https://aws.amazon.com/cloudwatch/
	•	Systems Manager Automation
  : https://aws.amazon.com/systems-manager/
AWS native services provide complete DR capability within the AWS ecosystem but do not extend to multi-cloud scenarios. Organisations using AWS and Azure must implement provider-agnostic solutions.
3.2 CloudEndure Disaster Recovery (AWS)
CloudEndure, acquired by AWS, provides automated DR with continuous replication and orchestrated failover. It implements block-level continuous replication, automated conversion to target region, orchestrated failover with configurable sequencing, and automated failback. The platform achieves RPO measured in seconds and RTO of minutes.
CloudEndure Disaster Recovery: https://aws.amazon.com/cloudendure-disaster-recovery/
3.3 Zerto (Multi-Cloud DR Platform)
Zerto provides enterprise-grade DR spanning VMware, AWS, Azure, Google Cloud, and IBM Cloud. It delivers continuous data protection with journal-based replication, cross-cloud replication, orchestrated failover with dependency mapping, near-zero RPO (typically under 5 seconds), and integrated non-disruptive DR testing.
Zerto Disaster Recovery: https://www.zerto.com/
3.4 Veeam Backup & Replication
Veeam provides backup, replication, and DR across on-premises, AWS, Azure, and GCP. Key capabilities include application-aware backup with transaction consistency, replication to remote sites or cloud providers, orchestrated failover with recovery plans, and instant recovery minimising RTO.
Veeam Backup & Replication: https://www.veeam.com/vm-backup-recovery-replication-software.html
3.5 Azure Site Recovery
Microsoft Azure Site Recovery provides DR as a service for applications running on Azure, VMware, Hyper-V, or physical servers. It delivers continuous replication, automated failover with customisable recovery plans, and integration with Azure Traffic Manager for traffic routing.
Azure Site Recovery: https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/products/site-recovery/
3.6 VMware Site Recovery Manager
VMware SRM provides policy-based replication between VMware sites, automated failover orchestration with customisable recovery plans, and non-disruptive testing validating DR procedures without impacting production.
VMware Site Recovery Manager: https://www.vmware.com/products/site-recovery-manager.html

4. Recommended Architecture: Harness + DR Services
4.1 Harness Role in a Complete DR Solution
Harness serves as the infrastructure deployment and continuous delivery layer. It ensures infrastructure parity across regions, enforces policy compliance, and deploys applications consistently. These are the foundation on which operational resilience systems then operate.
Harness Responsibilities (within a complete architecture):
	•	Infrastructure as Code management deploying consistent infrastructure across all regions
	•	Application deployment pipelines ensuring identical application versions in all regions
	•	Policy enforcement validating compliance before infrastructure provisioning
	•	Drift detection identifying and remediating configuration divergence between regions
	•	Chaos Engineering (HCE) validating that the DR mechanisms built with other tools work correctly
	•	SRM providing SLO compliance dashboards and error budget reporting to satisfy regulatory documentation requirements
Required Complementary Services:
	•	AWS Route 53 or Azure Traffic Manager — health-check-driven traffic routing and automated failover
	•	Aurora Global Database or Cosmos DB — cross-region data replication achieving RPO < 1 second
	•	Redis Global Datastore — session state synchronisation
	•	Datadog or New Relic — comprehensive observability and external alerting
	•	PagerDuty — incident management and escalation
	•	AWS Systems Manager or Azure Automation — automated incident response runbooks
4.2 Operational Workflow: Normal vs Disaster Scenario
Normal Operations: Harness manages infrastructure and deployments. Route 53 directs traffic to Frankfurt. Aurora replicates to secondary regions continuously. Datadog monitors application health and feeds SLO data to Harness SRM dashboards.
Deployment Event: An engineer commits an infrastructure change. Harness detects the change, executes the pipeline, deploys to Frankfurt, validates using Datadog metrics via SRM, deploys to Ireland, validates, then deploys to Amsterdam. Infrastructure consistency is maintained across all regions.
Disaster Scenario (3:00 AM, Frankfurt fails): Route 53 health checks detect failure within 30 seconds. CloudWatch alarms trigger a PagerDuty incident. Route 53 removes Frankfurt from DNS, routing traffic to Ireland. A Systems Manager runbook promotes Aurora Ireland to primary. Total RTO: approximately 3 minutes. Harness is completely uninvolved in this sequence — it plays no role in failure detection or response. The DR architecture operates autonomously.

