6. DORA Compliance Considerations
6.1 Article 28: ICT Concentration Risk
DORA Article 28 requires financial entities to identify and assess concentration risk from ICT third-party service providers. Harness's IaCM capabilities support multi-cloud deployment, which can reduce single-provider infrastructure dependency. However, infrastructure provisioning alone does not satisfy Article 28.
Regulatory compliance requires demonstrating operational continuity capability during provider failures. This means documented RTO and RPO measurements from actual testing, automated failover validated through regular DR drills, continuous monitoring and incident response, and exit planning showing ability to migrate between providers. Harness contributes to infrastructure portability but provides none of the testing, failover, or incident response capabilities needed for compliance demonstration.
DORA Regulation (EU) 2022/2554: https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2022/2554/oj
6.2 RTO/RPO Documentation Requirements
DORA and EBA guidelines require financial institutions to define and document recovery time objectives and recovery point objectives for critical systems based on actual measurements, not theoretical estimates. Measuring RTO and RPO requires operational testing of complete failure scenarios — detection time, DNS failover propagation, database promotion, application validation. Harness does not provide a framework for this testing. Harness Chaos Engineering can simulate faults, but the resulting measurements of RTO and RPO depend on the failover mechanisms built outside Harness.
EBA ICT Risk Guidelines: https://www.eba.europa.eu/regulation-and-policy/internal-governance/guidelines-on-ict-and-security-risk-management
6.3 Operational Resilience Testing
Regulators require periodic DR testing demonstrating operational continuity. Effective testing requires orchestration of complete failover workflows: scheduled failover to secondary region, validation of application functionality, measurement of RTO and RPO achieved, documentation of issues, failback to primary, and post-test reporting. Harness Chaos Engineering (https://developer.harness.io/docs/chaos-engineering/) is well-suited to be the fault injection and validation layer in such tests. However, the test itself is only meaningful if the underlying failover infrastructure — Route 53, Aurora Global, Systems Manager — has been built and is operational.

7. Conclusions and Recommendations
7.1 Summary of Findings
Harness is a world-class software delivery platform. Its own description as 'a unified, end-to-end AI software delivery platform to manage the SDLC' is accurate and internally consistent. Its module catalogue covers the complete SDLC from code management through deployment and cost optimisation. Two of its modules — Chaos Engineering and Service Reliability Management — touch the boundary between SDLC and operations, but they validate and measure resilience; they do not implement it.
Disaster recovery is not part of the SDLC. It is an operational concern that engages when the SDLC has concluded and the software is running in production. The capabilities required for DR — external health observation, automated traffic failover, continuous data replication, and autonomous incident response — are entirely absent from Harness. This is not a gap or deficiency in Harness; it is simply outside the platform's designed purpose.
Organisations that deploy Harness without complementary DR infrastructure will have excellent automation for delivering software to multiple regions. They will have no automated capability to maintain service continuity when those regions fail.
7.2 Recommendations for Santander CIB
Immediate (Next 30 Days):
	•	Formally communicate to all stakeholders that Harness provides SDLC automation, not disaster recovery
	•	Audit which DR capabilities currently exist: health monitoring, traffic routing, data replication, incident response
	•	Identify Tier 1 applications that lack automated failover and quantify the compliance exposure
Short-Term (3–6 Months):
	•	Implement Route 53 health checks and failover policies for all Tier 1 and Tier 2 applications
	•	Configure Aurora Global Database or equivalent replication for critical databases
	•	Establish Datadog or equivalent continuous monitoring feeding Harness SRM for SLO dashboards
	•	Use Harness Chaos Engineering to validate the failover mechanisms once they are in place
Medium-Term (6–18 Months):
	•	Complete state synchronisation (Redis Global, S3 CRR) for full session continuity during failover
	•	Automate incident response runbooks using AWS Systems Manager or Azure Automation
	•	Run quarterly DR drills using Harness Chaos Engineering against the complete architecture
	•	Document actual RTO/RPO measurements from each test for DORA compliance reporting
7.3 The Right Mental Model
Think of Harness as the construction crew that builds and maintains the building to a consistent specification across all sites. Route 53, Aurora Global, and Datadog are the alarm systems, fire suppression, and emergency generators that keep people safe when something goes wrong. You need both. The construction crew cannot trigger the sprinklers, and the emergency systems do not build the building. Presenting Harness to the regulator as a DR solution would be equivalent to showing the fire marshal a floor plan and claiming it substitutes for a smoke detector.

